        <div id="protein-blend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is in the Protein Blend
          </h2>

          <p>
            Muscle Milk powders are not pure whey. Pro Series and Genuine both use a milk-protein blend, which per the labels we checked combines milk protein isolate or concentrate, calcium or sodium caseinate and whey protein concentrate, with whey isolate also listed in some Pro Series flavors. The exact order of ingredients varies by flavor and label version.
          </p>

          <p>
            That blend is not a weakness. Milk protein is roughly 80 percent casein and 20 percent whey, so these powders digest more slowly than a straight whey isolate. For a post-workout shake the difference is small in practice, and for a shake between meals or before bed a slower release is arguably an advantage. Research on muscle protein synthesis consistently finds that total daily protein matters far more than the exact type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the blend does matter is lactose and digestion. Concentrates and caseinates carry more lactose than isolates, so readers who bloat on regular milk may react to Genuine or Pro Series. If that is you, the 100% Whey, a pure isolate or one of the picks in our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey versus whey isolate</a> comparison is a safer start.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 100% Whey is the only straight whey product in the range, and Zero is built on milk protein too. None of the powders are vegan, and all carry a milk allergen warning. If you need dairy-free, Muscle Milk is simply the wrong brand, and our <a href="/sunwarrior-protein-powder-review/">Sunwarrior review</a> covers a plant-based alternative.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where the Calories Come From
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read the ingredient list on a Genuine tub and you will see maltodextrin, a non-dairy creamer blend and oils high up. Those are what give the shake its thick, milkshake-like texture, and they are also where most of the 270 to 280 calories per serving come from. Sugar is low, around 2 to 4 g depending on flavor, but carbohydrate is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pro Series carries some of the same texture ingredients, including maltodextrin and MCTs, which is why a two-scoop serving lands around 300 to 320 calories. The difference is that 50 g of protein accounts for about 200 of those calories, so the non-protein share is much smaller. Per gram of protein, Pro Series is the leaner of the two by a wide margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            For someone trying to gain weight, those extra calories are a feature. A hard gainer who struggles to eat enough can drink a Genuine shake with whole milk and add 400 to 500 calories without much effort. For that use, Muscle Milk competes with lighter gainers, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">best protein powder for weight gain</a> guide covers the heavier options.
          </p>

          <p>
            For someone cutting, the calculus flips. Every 100 calories of maltodextrin and creamer is 100 calories you could have spent on food. If you are in a calorie deficit, choose the 100% Whey or Pro Series at one scoop, or an isolate. Muscle Milk Zero is lower in calories, but you pay for that with less protein per serving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certification" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. NSF Certification
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is Muscle Milk's strongest selling point and the one most buyers overlook. Per the brand, its protein powders are NSF Certified for Sport, which means each certified product is screened for more than 270 substances banned in sport, checked for label claim accuracy and reviewed for contaminants. The program also audits the manufacturing facility.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tested athletes, from college players to anyone competing in a drug-tested federation, NSF Certified for Sport is the certification most governing bodies point to. It is unusual to find it on a powder at Muscle Milk's price. Many budget tubs carry no third-party certification at all, and many premium brands charge twice as much for the same seal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification status can change by flavor and size, and it is not a guarantee about every lot. If you are a tested athlete, look up the exact product and flavor in the NSF Certified for Sport database before you buy, and keep the lot number. The brand claim is a good sign, but the database is the record.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification is not the same as a clean label. NSF checks that the product contains what it says and nothing banned. It does not judge artificial sweeteners, maltodextrin or creamer. A powder can be certified and still not be the one you want to drink every day, which is why we rank Pro Series above Genuine despite both carrying the same claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Muscle Milk if you want a short ingredient list.</strong> Every flavored powder in the range uses acesulfame potassium and sucralose, and the larger tubs add maltodextrin, creamer and oils. If you prefer unflavored or stevia-sweetened powders, look at our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if you are lactose sensitive.</strong> The milk-protein and concentrate blends in Pro Series and Genuine are not low-lactose products. An isolate such as Dymatize ISO100, covered in our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a>, is the more comfortable choice for most people who react to dairy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Genuine if you are cutting</strong>, and skip Zero if cost per gram matters to you. Neither is a bad product, but each is built for a narrow use. Genuine is a calorie product, and Zero is a light shake that delivers barely half a normal post-workout dose per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if taste is your top priority.</strong> Buyer feedback on Pro Series flavor is mixed, with chocolate generally preferred over vanilla and strawberry. Genuine gets warmer reviews for creaminess. If you want a powder that buyers consistently rate as dessert-level, our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide is a better place to shop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Use It for Muscle Gain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most adults building muscle do well on roughly 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight per day, with benefits tapering above about 2.2 g per kg, according to a widely cited 2018 meta-analysis. A protein powder is just a convenient way to hit that number. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> walks through the maths for your weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use Pro Series as one scoop, not two, for most shakes. One scoop gives roughly 25 g of protein, which is the dose most studies use after training. Save the full 50 g serving for days when you are replacing a meal or you are a larger lifter with a high daily target. Splitting the serving doubles the life of the tub and halves the cost per shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use Genuine as a calorie tool. Mix two scoops with whole milk and a banana, and you have a 500-plus calorie shake for a hard gainer. Use 100% Whey as your daily scoop if you want a straight whey, and keep Zero for people who want a light protein drink rather than a muscle-building dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, the shake is a supplement to meals, not a replacement for them. People with kidney disease, those who are pregnant, and anyone on medication or with a milk allergy should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a high-protein powder. This page is label analysis, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

